@page "/learn/virtualize-variable-height"
@layout LearnLayout
@using NorthwindOps.Components.Guide.Demos.VariableHeight

<PageTitle>Lists with items of any height · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<style>
    .learn .chart-card { background: #fff; padding: 12px; }
    .learn .chart-card .height-chart { display: block; width: 100%; }
</style>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Lists with items of any height</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Your thread mixes one-line log entries with long emails. Can you virtualize it without the jumps?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Grids &amp; lists · Interactive Server · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> nothing for you to write. <code>Virtualize</code> itself got better at lists
        whose items vary in height. In .NET 10 the same markup worked, but the scrollbar jumped around and expanding
        a message moved the page.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a long support thread. Some messages are one line. Others are long emails. Long messages are cut
        to three lines at first. Each has a <b>Show more</b> button, and <b>Expand all</b> above the list opens them all.
    </p>
    <p>Here's the list. It's plain <code>Virtualize</code>, with nothing new:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The list, in .NET 10 and .NET 11 alike</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;div class="thread"&gt;   <span class="dim">@@* height: 440px; overflow-y: auto *@@</span>
    <span class="err">&lt;Virtualize Items="messages" Context="m" ItemSize="80"&gt;</span>
        &lt;article class="msg"&gt;...&lt;/article&gt;
    &lt;/Virtualize&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        On .NET 10 this list's scrollbar jumped as you scrolled. .NET 11 fixed that inside <code>Virtualize</code>,
        so the old behaviour can't run on this site. Instead, we ran a small .NET 10 app with this markup, scrolled
        it down 400px at a time, and measured the list's total height after each step. The thread has 420
        messages: one-line log entries mixed with long emails.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The list's height while you scroll, on .NET 10" Url="demo/variable-height/incident/net10"
                Label=".NET 10 · measured" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Nothing is added or removed, so the line should be flat. Instead it zigzags by up to 12,550px. The
            scrollbar's thumb is sized from that height, so it grows, shrinks and jumps under your finger.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>It guessed from what was on screen</h2>
    <p>
        <code>Virtualize</code> only renders the items you can see, plus a few either side. For the rest it uses two
        empty blocks, called spacers. Their height comes from an average item height. In .NET 10 that average came
        only from the items on screen right now:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">How .NET 10 sized the messages you can't see</span>
        <pre class="snip">in view: mostly log lines    <span class="dim">small average</span>  → list height <span class="err">shrinks</span>
scroll on: a few emails      <span class="dim">large average</span>  → list height <span class="err">grows</span>
scroll on: log lines again   <span class="dim">small average</span>  → list height <span class="err">shrinks</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        So every time you scrolled to a different mix, the whole list changed size. That's the zigzag in Fig. 1.
        Sometimes the list even jumped back up while you scrolled down.
    </p>
    <p>
        .NET 10 also set <code>overflow-anchor: none</code> on the scrolling box. That turns off the browser's
        "keep my place" feature. So when a message above you grew, it pushed what you were reading down.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Same code, on .NET 11</h2>
    <p>
        There's no new API. Keep the markup above: a scrolling box with a height, and messages at their natural
        size. <code>ItemSize</code> is now just a first guess.
        .NET 11 averages every item it has measured so far, so the size settles down. It also leaves the browser's
        "keep my place" feature on, so what you're reading stays put. Here's the same app, built for .NET 11 and
        measured the same way:
    </p>

    <figure class="fig">
        <figcaption class="fig-title">Fig. 2 · The list's height while you scroll, on .NET 11</figcaption>
        <div class="chart-card"><HeightChart Heights="Measured.Net11" Colour="#1a7f37" /></div>
        <p class="fig-cap">
            Same axes as Fig. 1. The height creeps up a little as .NET 11 measures more messages, then settles. The
            scrollbar stays put.
        </p>
    </figure>

    <p>And here's the list live, on this site's real support thread.</p>

    <DemoFigure Number="3" Title="The list, live on .NET 11" Url="demo/variable-height/incident"
                Label=".NET 11 · live">
        <Caption>
            Scroll the list down and back up. The <b>Scroll range</b> under it is the list's height, as in Figs. 1
            and 2: it barely moves. Then, part way down, press <b>Expand all</b>. The message at the top stays where it is.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>All three use the same <code>Virtualize</code> markup. Here's Fig. 3's page in full:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/VariableHeight/IncidentThread.razor" Feature="virtualize-variable-height">
        <Caption>
            The highlighted <code>Virtualize</code> line is where this happens, but you don't change it. It's the
            same as in .NET 10. What changed is how .NET 11 measures items and keeps your place.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Fixed heights, or no virtualizing</h2>
    <p>To stop the jumping, apps gave up either natural heights or virtualizing:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Two ways round it in .NET 10, and what they cost</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;Virtualize Items="messages" <span class="err">ItemSize="120"</span>&gt;
    <span class="dim">@@* 120px, overflow: hidden *@@</span>
    &lt;article class="preview"&gt;...
        &lt;button&gt;Read all&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/Virtualize&gt;
&lt;MessageDialog Message="open" /&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">every item is the height Virtualize expects, so nothing jumps</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ a one-liner gets an email's box, and long messages are read in a dialog</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;button @@onclick="() =&gt; shown += 50"&gt;
    Show 50 older messages
&lt;/button&gt;
@@foreach (var m in messages.<span class="err">TakeLast(shown)</span>)</pre>
                <span class="why">nothing is virtualized, so heights can be anything</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ every message you load stays on the page, so a long thread still gets slow</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>It measures, and keeps your place</h2>
    <p>
        On .NET 11 the scrollbar stays steady as you scroll (Fig. 2), and expanding messages above you leaves what
        you're reading in place. Try it in Fig. 3.
    </p>
    <p>
        If you used fixed heights, drop the fixed-height box, its <code>overflow: hidden</code> and the dialog. Let
        messages be their natural size. You can leave <code>ItemSize</code> in place: it becomes the first guess.
    </p>
    <p>
        <b>One thing changes when you upgrade:</b> more items render. <code>OverscanCount</code> now defaults to 15
        either side, up from 3. If your items are slow to render, set it back to 3. QuickGrid still defaults to 3.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Give it a scrolling box.</b> <code>Virtualize</code> scrolls inside the nearest parent with <code>overflow-y: auto</code> or <code>scroll</code>. Give that box a fixed height, like the demo's 440px.</li>
        <li><b>Close, not exact.</b> The list's height still shifts a little while .NET 11 measures more messages. Very mixed heights are harder to estimate.</li>
        <li><b>Refreshing starts over.</b> <code>RefreshDataAsync()</code> and changing <code>ItemSize</code> both reset the average.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
